  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "Let $\\mathrm{A}=\\left(\\begin{array}{ll}\\mathrm{a} & \\mathrm{b} \\\\ c & \\mathrm{~d}\\end{array}\\right)$ be a $2 \\times 2$ real matrix with $\\operatorname{det} \\mathrm{A}=1$. If the equation $\\operatorname{det}\\left(\\mathrm{A}-\\lambda \\mathrm{I}{2}\\right)=0$ has imaginary roots $\\left(\\mathrm{I}{2}\\right.$ be the\r\nIdentity matrix of order 2), then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "8uOtk",
      "text": "$(a+d)^{2}    < 4$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "hqe0S",
      "text": "$(a+d)^{2}=4$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "QFUPT",
      "text": "$(a+d)^{2}>4$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "MGFYH",
      "text": "$(a+d)^{2}=16$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hint: \r\n$|\\mathrm{A}|=0 \\therefore \\mathrm{ad}-\\mathrm{bc}=1$\r\n$\\left|\\mathrm{~A}-\\lambda \\mathrm{I}_{2}\\right|=0$\r\n$\\left|\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathrm{a}-\\lambda & \\mathrm{b} \\\\ \\mathrm{c} & \\mathrm{d}-\\lambda\\end{array}\\right|=0$\r\n$\\therefore \\mathrm{ad}-(\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{d}) \\lambda+\\lambda^{2}-\\mathrm{bc}=0$\r\n$\\lambda^{2}-(\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{d}) \\lambda+1=0$\r\n$\\therefore(\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{d})^{2}    < 4$",
    "image": null
  },
